 17592186045417] [17592186482483 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ce36ee07-fbbc-5e29-8d0c-a6748c1dafd2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482498 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186482115 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482498 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186482137 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482498 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186482267 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482498 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186482275 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482498 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186482308 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482498 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482498 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482498 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186482275 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482498 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f5006c5b-f6bb-5a8a-aaf0-fd1de2ee6fd8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482504 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482416 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482504 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161787" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482504 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482504 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/61/61bd212879bc2f37dba7637bb3b126e33c5f7b0fbebd5a676874d9b3f6bc38da.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=6y$ for $x\\ge 0,$ shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482504 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0136e908-03ab-5353-9218-7031e36c58ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482504 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-half of the sphere of radius $3$ centered at $(0,3,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=6y$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =6y \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =6\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho -6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/f5/f52fe1c2e73dcbe972d72d263a5a43f2c99dbd573395fbf6e3a3b78b46e1f461.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)^{2}+(\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{{\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ}\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}[\\rho ]_{\\rho =0}^{\\rho =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}\\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ-0)\\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }dϕ\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,d\\theta  \\\\  & =6[ϕ]_{ϕ=0}^{ϕ=\\pi }\\cdot [-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =\\pi /2} \\\\  & =6(\\pi -0)\\cdot (-0+1) \\\\  & =6\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482510 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482434 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482510 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161758" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482510 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482510 #edb/ref 1071 "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.$\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}x+y\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482510 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1521c62d-edb7-5cf4-9412-1fda40e8b968" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482510 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $1$ centered at the origin. \nAs a result, in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=x+y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ+\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x+y\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\rho }^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482512 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482512 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161759" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482512 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482512 #edb/ref 1071 "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=5$ for $y\\ge 0.$\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482512 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "164ec461-433f-5d34-8b46-e1e7c87d7fe4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482512 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-half of the sphere of radius $\\sqrt{5}$ centered at the origin.\n\n![](images/9c/9ca3a428d54a15b11c4c5bd95039df6a0b2d9f4363dba7034c51edb2b6f8fd27.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le \\sqrt{5},\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\rho }^{2}} \\\\  & =\\rho \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\rho \\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482521 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482406 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482521 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161755" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482521 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482521 #edb/ref 1071 "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.$\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}xz({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482521 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "31ed4558-6b89-5259-81f7-c1113b514f8f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482521 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $\\frac{1}{2}$ centered at the origin. \nAs a result, in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le \\frac{1}{2},\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=xz({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot \\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2} \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}xz({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{\\rho }^{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482535 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482401 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482535 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161789" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482535 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482535 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/08/085e28508da725e445c410fedc08a1992dccc5e0a7b99b3248182f58ffae2c07.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4z,$ shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482535 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4913e4d1-197e-5ba9-a574-72530dddbf74" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482535 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $2$ centered at $(0,0,2).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4z$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =4z \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =4\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/8a/8a730a72fe9f3f69878ef647215e8ed05e913f23bf8e0fab01bad6271e860335.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{\\rho }^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\rho }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{\\rho }\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ{[\\frac{1}{2}{\\rho }^{2}]}_{\\rho =0}^{\\rho =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ}\\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }8\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,dϕ\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }d\\theta  \\\\  & =8{[-\\frac{1}{3}{cos}^{3}⁡ϕ]}_{ϕ=0}^{ϕ=\\pi /2}\\cdot [\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}(0-1)\\cdot (2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482536 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482483 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482536 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161774" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482536 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482536 #edb/ref 1071 "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=6$ for $x\\le 0$ and $z\\le 0.$\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482536 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4a047214-1415-5946-9ec9-da29ccac571a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482536 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-quarter of the sphere of radius $\\sqrt{6}$ centered at the origin.\n\n![](images/02/02834e98afa14d5193e5d560f85457e73caee7f5938af5ac8b3fb607165aad53.png)\n\n \nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le \\sqrt{6},\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)^{2} \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{6}}{\\rho }^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482552 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482412 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482552 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161921" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482552 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482552 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/54/54ff64195ccfadddfe2b3ca0bb9e193147fedae20d8dae6a53a5ecddc3c26f5d.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=-16y$ for $x\\le 0$ and $z\\ge 0,$ shown above. \nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482552 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "75e6fbad-2ffa-554a-b73e-206d1de35163" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482552 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$  represents one-quarter of the sphere of radius $8$ centered at $(0,-8,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=-16y$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\