atorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot \\rho  \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\rho }^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482585 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482585 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161922" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482585 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482585 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/cf/cf44412afd6f2a8eaab468676b4f6a9828f5000eec287234f9834a41e6816d5a.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=20x$ for $z\\le 0,$ shown above.\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482585 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ba16213a-d9f2-5675-b515-f66161bd7267" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482585 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $